# Does a change of state reach the 3D, and what else could?

Audit document. Covers the two questions in `scripts/development/Goal`. It
replaces the implementation design that stood here previously — that document
was the plan for building the feed, and the feed is built.

Every factual claim below was verified either against the working tree or
against the live Prometheus over `ssh K3s-server` (NodePort 30990) on
2026-08-23. Claims I could not verify are marked as assumptions, with the
measurement that would settle them. Line references are to the tree as it stands
on `feat-tagging-the-codes`.

---

# Part 0 — Summary

**Goal step 1 — yes, with two qualifications.** A metric change repaints the 3D
scene without a remount and without dropping any strip's rotation. Three visual
channels are live: status colour, rotation rate, and the stale ghost. But the
module *set* is frozen after the first poll (Part 2.1), and every numeric readout
in the panel that carries a "Driven by live data" badge is showing a first-poll
value (Part 2.2). Both are client-side; neither is a rendering problem.

**Goal step 2 — the feed is over-supplied, not under-supplied.** 91 channels
arrive per snapshot. The client keeps 7 scalars per module and discards
`channels[]` wholesale at `live/adapt.ts:45`. Of the 7, three drive something you
can see, one drives audio, and three drive nothing at all. Twenty-one activity
channels have no representation anywhere in the UI, and not one raw value with
its unit is displayed on any surface.

So the answer to "can more data be presented" is yes, easily — but the binding
constraint is display surface, not Prometheus. The cluster holds 6412 metric
names and the catalogue uses about 25; adding more PromQL before adding somewhere
to put it just widens a payload nothing reads. Part 5 ranks the candidates
anyway, because several are cheap and two of them fix channels that are currently
wired to the wrong thing.

