# Part 1 — Does a change of state update the UI live? (Goal step 1)

## 1.1 The path, end to end

```
data-provider/SystemCore/queries.ts:40    POLL_MS = 20_000, refetchInterval
  hooks/SystemCore/useLive.ts:111         readings = useMemo(readingsOf(snapshot), [snapshot])
    live/adapt.ts:66                      readingsOf  -> Map<id, Reading>
      live/adapt.ts:45                    readingOf   -> one Reading
  Dialog.tsx:143                          readings passed to <Scene>
    Scene.tsx:189                         strips = useMemo(..., [modules, readings, ...])
      scene/resolve.ts:91                 stripSpec(m, rt, live)
      scene/resolve.ts:82                 appearanceOf(m, live)
        live/bind.ts                      the policy layer
      scene/materials.ts:218              mats.forModule(color, opacity, gain)
    Strip.tsx:175                         re-renders, keyed by module id
```

Every link holds. The snapshot object identity changes on every response —
`cacheAgeMs` is recomputed per hit at `snapshot.ts:169` and `collectedAt` per
refresh — so React Query's structural sharing cannot short-circuit the `readings`
memo, and `readingsOf` builds fresh `Reading` objects regardless. The `strips`
memo lists `readings` in its dependency array, so it recomputes; `Strip` is keyed
by `spec.id`, so it re-renders rather than remounts.

The three animation invariants documented at `Strip.tsx:4-27` all still hold, so
nothing about a poll disturbs a strip's spin phase:

1. `rotation.y` is never a prop — set once in the callback ref at `Strip.tsx:116`,
   thereafter only by `useFrame`.
2. Strips are keyed by module id at `Scene.tsx:253`, so new props do not remount
   and never touch a sibling.
3. The live angle is parked into a ref-held `Map` every frame
   (`useModules.ts:160`), so even a genuine remount resumes rather than snapping.

## 1.2 What actually moves

`Reading` (`live/bind.ts:60`) carries seven fields. Here is what each one reaches:

| Field | Binding | Reaches | Live? |
|---|---|---|---|
| `status` | `effectiveStatus` → `statusColor` / `statusGain` | material colour, band emissive gain | **yes, visual** |
| `rate` | `boundSpeed` (`bind.ts:233`) | strip rotation rate | **yes, visual** |
| `stale` | `boundOpacity` (`bind.ts:271`) | opacity → 0.3 ghost | **yes, visual** |
| `health` | `boundLevel` (`bind.ts:300`) | drone level via `ToneBus` | yes, audio only |
| `load` | `effectiveProgress` → `readoutOf` | `resolve.ts:122` — **no caller** | no |
| `up` | — | documented "for display"; never read | no |
| `overflow` | lane, at reconcile time | `<Contact>` is commented out (`Strip.tsx:267-281`) | no |

`readoutOf`, `effectiveHealth` and `effectiveProgress` are referenced only from
`live/__tests__/bind.spec.ts`. `resolve.ts:112-127` says "the panel is the only
reader" — the panel does not read them either. They are correct, tested, and
dead.

**Status colour is the channel that actually reads as live**, because it is a
discrete jump between five saturated palette entries. The other two are weaker
than they look:

- **Rotation** is deliberately a narrow band: `RATE_QUIET = 0.85` to
  `RATE_BUSY = 1.55` against `RATE_FULL = 200` events/s (`bind.ts:144-147`). On
  this cluster the five real throughput channels sit at 3.2–53.4 events/s, which
  maps to just 1.04×–1.38× of the authored speed; the two reading zero sit at the
  0.85× floor. Redis going from its current 53/s to a
  saturating 200/s would move its strip from 0.517 to 0.581 rev/s — a 12% change,
  spread over a 20-second tick. It is a background level, not a visible event.
  That is the design working as documented (`bind.ts:131-143` argues explicitly
  against one strip visibly turning while twenty-seven appear stopped), but it is
  worth stating plainly: nobody will *see* the spin change.
- **Only 7 of 28 modules have a live rate at all.** The other 21 have no
  `throughput` channel, so `rate` is null and they turn at their authored speed
  forever. Of those 7, two are bound to something that is not a rate (Part 5.1),
  leaving **5 strips out of 28 whose rotation responds to real throughput**.

## 1.3 What a tick costs

Nothing, in the steady state, which is the point of the codomain invariant at
`live/bind.ts:1-33`. Verified by reading the caches rather than by measurement:

- `MaterialRegistry` is keyed by `kind|color|opacity|gain` (`materials.ts:195`).
  Colour comes from `status`, whose codomain is the six keys of `data/status.ts`;
  opacity is the authored value or the single `STALE_OPACITY` constant. So a tick
  that changes no status returns the same material objects and R3F writes nothing.
- `boundSpeed` and `boundLevel` round to three decimals (`PRECISION`,
  `bind.ts:156`), so an unchanged sample produces a bit-identical number and R3F's
  prop diff skips it.
- Geometry `args` arrays are rebuilt per render but compared element-wise, so
  equal values reconstruct nothing.

`Scene.tsx:216-224` logs `materials`, `geometries`, `textures` and `programs`
behind `VITE_ENABLE_LOGGER`; all four flat across ticks is the passing condition.

## 1.4 Reduced motion still updates

Under `prefers-reduced-motion` the canvas runs `frameloop="demand"`
(`Scene.tsx:286`), so nothing repaints unless something asks. A poll does ask:
R3F's `applyProps` calls `invalidateInstance` after writing a changed prop, and
`invalidateInstance` calls `state.invalidate()` whenever `frames === 0` — verified
in `node_modules/@react-three/fiber/dist/events-d0566a2e.cjs.dev.js:787` and
`:805` at @react-three/fiber 8.18.0. So a material swap forces a frame.

What is lost under reduced motion is the rotation channel (no `useFrame`) and the
audio channel (`bus` is null at `Scene.tsx:141`). Colour and opacity still track.

## 1.5 Hand edits and the poll do not fight

Worth recording because it is the property that makes the panel safe. The poll
never writes an authored field: `useModules.ts:14-21` reconciles adds and removes
only, and placement hints are consumed exactly once when a module is first added.
Live values modulate at read time in `bind.ts` rather than being merged into the
module. There is therefore no field-level conflict to resolve, and `BINDINGS`
(`bind.ts:117`) locks only the four paths the poll owns.

